<section class="sp-sec"><h2>The setting</h2><p>Two years into the great uprising of the enslaved in the French colony of Saint-Domingue, Toussaint, a formerly enslaved coachman, had become a commander of the insurgent forces, then allied with Spain. On the day the French commissioner Sonthonax proclaimed the end of slavery in the north of the colony, Toussaint issued a proclamation of his own, signed with the name he had recently taken: Louverture.</p></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>Key passage</h2><blockquote class="sp-quote"><p>I want Liberty and Equality to reign in Saint-Domingue. I am working to make that happen. Unite yourselves to us, brothers, and fight with us for the same cause.</p></blockquote><p class="sp-source">Proclamation of Camp Turel, 29 August 1793, translated from the French.</p><div class="sp-full"><button type="button" class="sp-share" data-share-text="“I want Liberty and Equality to reign in Saint-Domingue. I am working to make that happen. Unite yourselves to us, brothers, and fight with us for the same cause.” — Toussaint Louverture, Toussaint Louverture at Camp Turel" data-share-img="/images/og/speech-toussaint-louverture-at-camp-turel.jpg">Share this quote</button></div></section>
<section class="sp-sec"><h2>What followed</h2><p>In 1794, after the French Republic abolished slavery throughout its colonies, Toussaint changed sides and drove out the Spanish and the British, and by 1801 he governed the whole island. Napoleon sent an army to restore French control; Toussaint was seized and died in a French prison in 1803. His lieutenant Jean-Jacques Dessalines completed the revolution, and Haiti declared its independence on 1 January 1804.</p><div class="sp-led"><h3 class="sp-led-k">What it led to</h3><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">First Empire of Haiti</a></div></div></section>
